    english_customer_response_template: 'Hi {{USERNAME}}, the Windows Hello PIN setup was failing on your computer. The PIN depends on a security chip on the motherboard being ready and on your device registration with the company network; after the recent BIOS update, that chip had been reset, so the old PIN no longer existed and the setup could not complete. We prepared the security chip again, verified the device registration and the network time, and removed the leftover PIN data so the wizard could start fresh. Your new PIN is set and sign-in works with it. Please restart once and confirm you can sign in with the PIN. If the message returns after a BIOS change, reply to ticket {{TICKET_ID}}.',
    english_technical_explanation: 'Windows Hello PIN provisioning failure on {{HOSTNAME}} at {{TIMESTAMP}}: setup failing with "something went wrong" and PIN lost after a BIOS update. Prerequisites verified: system clock within Kerberos tolerance and device joined with PRT present (dsregcmd /status showed AzureAdJoined YES, AzureAdPrt YES). Root cause: the BIOS update cleared the TPM, invalidating the previous PIN protector; Get-Tpm showed TpmReady False, corrected with Initialize-Tpm and a reboot. The stale Hello key container (C:\\Windows\\ServiceProfiles\\LocalService\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Ngc) was removed as admin with the user session closed, allowing clean re-provisioning. The biometric service was verified (Get-Service WbioSrvc) for fingerprint users. Verification: PIN created without error and PIN sign-in works after a full restart.',
